\DOC X86_ADD_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC

\TYPE {X86_ADD_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> thm -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Derives a full subroutine-level {ensures} specification — one starting at the
entry point and ending at the saved return address, with the stack pointer
popped — from a core specification of the same code plus its execution/length
data, usually closing the goal outright.

\DESCRIBE
{execth : thm * (thm option array)} is the result of making an executable
description of the machine code, pairing the code-length theorem with the
per-instruction decoding array; {coreth : thm} is the already-proved core
correctness theorem for the same code, whose pre/postconditions and {MAYCHANGE}
frame are lifted to the returning specification. The goal has the form {A ?- t}
where {t} is a universally quantified (optionally under an outer existential)
implication whose conclusion is an {ensures} statement about the returning
subroutine. Normally the tactic discharges such a goal completely. In one
situation — when the specification is existentially quantified over an
event-trace function — it instead strips and instantiates the universals and
the hypotheses, leaving one goal:
{
   A ?- t
   =====================================  X86_ADD_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC execth coreth
   A, z1, ..., zp ?- t'
}
\noindent where {z1 ... zp} are the unfolded nonoverlapping side conditions,
the loaded-code and saved-return-address facts, the core theorem's trace facts,
and {read}/{MAYCHANGE} facts about the final state, and {t'} is the remaining
existential trace obligation with the concrete event list substituted.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes (zero or one subgoal), on
goals of this subroutine-wrapping shape and with no prior assumptions.

\ENDDOC
